## 1. Lane 1 — model discrepancy — `ModelDiscrepancy.lean`

The Stage-6 observation model was `y = f_θ(x) + ε`.  Stage 7 introduces the
missing term:

```
y = f_θ(x) + δ(x) + ε.
```

| Statement | Kind | Content |
|---|---|---|
| `discrepancy`, `AdequateOn`, `Generates` | definition | `δ = truth − model`; adequacy on a set of times; data generated by truth plus bounded error |
| `truth_eq_model_add_discrepancy` | theorem | the decomposition is exact by construction |
| `bentData_pins_down_two` | theorem | a *precise* fit: the surviving region of the linear family is the single point `θ = 2` |
| **`fit_is_not_adequacy`** | theorem (negative) | that same `θ` is inadequate at an untested time — **parameter fit ⇏ model adequacy** |
| **`fit_precise_yet_arbitrarily_inadequate`** | theorem (negative) | the surviving region is a point and the discrepancy at an untested time can be made arbitrarily large |
| **`discrepancy_destroys_nominal_containment`** | theorem (negative) | unmodelled discrepancy larger than the declared tolerance drops the generating parameter out of the surviving region |
| `containment_of_small_discrepancy` | theorem | and is restored exactly when `‖δ‖ + noise ≤ tol` |
| **`silent_coverage_failure`** | theorem (negative) | the surviving region is nonempty — nothing signals a refutation — and yet it *excludes* the parameter that generated the data |
| `inflate`, `inflated_tolerance_contains_the_truth` | definition / theorem | inflating tolerances by a discrepancy bound restores containment … |
| `inflation_widens_the_region`, `inflation_strictly_widens` | theorem | … and the cost in region width is proved, not hand-waved |
| `every_parameter_is_consistent_with_some_discrepancy` | theorem (negative) | with `δ` unconstrained, *no* datum constrains `θ`: discrepancy must be bounded to be useful |
| `bounded_discrepancy_is_still_not_identified` | theorem (negative) | and even bounded `δ` leaves `θ` unidentified |

This is the requested boundary: *wrong model + flexible parameter ⇒ apparently
precise wrong inference*, with the "apparently precise" part proved as a
singleton surviving region.

## 1b. Lane 1, probabilistic half — `DiscrepancyCoverage.lean`

Lane 1 above is deterministic: it is about the *surviving region*.  The boxed
roadmap claim — *unmodelled discrepancy can destroy nominal coverage* — is
about a frequentist certificate, and is proved here against the Stage-6
`CoverageSemantics` machinery.

| Statement | Kind | Content |
|---|---|---|
| `coverageProb_eq_zero_of_always_missing` | theorem | the coverage-zero pole, dual to Stage 6's `coverageProb_eq_one_iff` |
| **`discrepancy_destroys_nominal_coverage`** | theorem (negative) | the *same* `toleranceProcedure` that Stage 6 certified as coverage-one has coverage **`0`** at the generating parameter once the data are off by more than the tolerance |
| **`no_positive_level_under_discrepancy`** | theorem (negative) | so it satisfies **no** positive `HasLevel`: the certificate is void, not degraded |
| `discrepancy_coverage_of_bounded_noise` | theorem | quantitative sufficient condition `tol + noiseBound < |δ(time)|` |
| `inflated_tolerance_restores_full_coverage` | theorem | inflating the tolerance restores coverage one, at the width cost proved in lane 1 |
| **`nominal_coverage_can_be_destroyed`** | theorem | a concrete witness: one declared family, one declared tolerance, coverage `1` on model-generated data and `0` on data bent by `δ` |

The moral, as a theorem: a coverage certificate is never a property of the
procedure alone — it is a property of the procedure *together with* the
assumption that the model is adequate.

## 2. Lane 2 — vector states, parameters and controls — `VectorDynamics.lean`

`ẋ = f(x, θ, u, t)` with `x ∈ ℝⁿ`, `θ ∈ ℝᵖ`, `u ∈ ℝᵐ`; observation
`y = h(x, θ)`.

| Statement | Kind | Content |
|---|---|---|
| `VecSystem n p m` | interface (record) | vector field with *certified* partials `f_x`, `f_θ` carried as fields |
| `IsVecTrajectory`, `IsSensitivityColumn` | definition | the trajectory, and a column of `S(t) = ∂x(t)/∂θ` satisfying `Ṡ = f_x S + f_θ` |
| **`sensitivity_column_unique`** | theorem | the variational equation plus `S(0) = 0` determines the column |
| `sensitivity_column_bound` | theorem | a Grönwall bound on its growth |
| `sensitivityMatrix`, `outputJacobian` | definition | the assembled `S(t)` and the measurement Jacobian |
| **`output_sensitivity_chain_rule`** | theorem | `∂y/∂θ = h_x S + h_θ` |
| `unexcited_parameter_is_invisible` | theorem | a parameter the field does not excite has zero sensitivity column |
| `unexcited_parameter_makes_information_singular` | theorem | and therefore makes the lane-3 information matrix singular |
| `information_of_trajectory_design` | definition/theorem | the bridge from a trajectory design to `JᵀWJ` |

**Obligation, stated honestly.** `IsSensitivityColumn` is a *hypothesis* about
`(x, S)`, not a derivation: the theorem that the parameter-derivative of the
flow satisfies the variational equation (differentiability of the flow in `θ`)
is **not** proved.  Stage 6 carried the same gap in the scalar case; Stage 7
generalises the statement, not the derivation.

## 3. Lane 3 — information matrix and experiment design — `InformationMatrix.lean`

| Statement | Kind | Content |
|---|---|---|
| `infoMatrix J W = JᵀWJ` | definition | deliberately **not** called Fisher information |
| `infoMatrix_quadratic` | theorem | `vᵀFv = (Jv)ᵀW(Jv)` |
| `zero_information_iff_invisible` | theorem | `vᵀFv = 0 ↔ Jv = 0` under a positive-definite weight |
| **`singular_iff_invisible_direction`** | theorem | `det F = 0 ↔ ∃ v ≠ 0, Jv = 0` |
| `infoOf_union`, `infoOf_insert`, `more_measurements_never_lose_information` | theorem | information is monotone in the measurement set (in the quadratic-form order) |
| `dCrit`, `aCrit`, `EigLower` | definition | all three roadmap criteria: `det F`, `tr(F⁻¹)`, and a certified `λ_min` lower bound |
| `eigLower_gives_derivative_lower_bound` | theorem | what a spectral lower bound buys — it is exactly the hypothesis the identifiability-radius theorem consumes |
| **`aCrit_of_singular`, `aCrit_ranks_a_singular_design_best`** | theorem (negative) | `tr(F⁻¹)` reports `0` for a *singular* design, so minimising it without a nonsingularity check selects the unidentifiable experiment |
| `best_design_by_det_is_identifiable_if_any_is` | theorem | selecting by `det F` finds an identifiable design whenever one exists |
| **`criteria_disagree`, `all_three_criteria_disagree`** | theorem (negative) | `det F`, `tr(F⁻¹)` and `λ_min(F)` rank two designs in opposite orders: there is no single "the" optimal experiment |
| `information_sees_only_the_jacobian_and_weight` | theorem | the claim boundary, as a theorem: `F` is a function of `(J, W)` only |

The name is `infoMatrix`, not `fisherInformation`, precisely because no
likelihood is declared; nothing in the corpus licenses the Cramér–Rao reading.

## 4. Lane 4 — correlated uncertainty — `CorrelatedUncertainty.lean`

`Σ_y = J Σ_x Jᵀ`, and the split into `Σ Jᵢ²σᵢ²` plus `2 Σ_{i<j} JᵢJⱼ Cov(xᵢ,xⱼ)`.

| Statement | Kind | Content |
|---|---|---|
| `bil`, `crossTerms`, `totalVariance_split`, `totalVariance_two` | definition / theorem | the exact decomposition into diagonal and cross terms |
| `outputVariance_eq_totalVariance` | theorem | agreement with the Stage-6 budget on the diagonal case |
| `correlation_error_bound_two` | theorem | how wrong the diagonal answer can be, in terms of the off-diagonal entry |
| `IsAttribution`, `symmetricAttribution`, `firstInputAttribution` | definition | two different conventions that both sum to the true variance |
| **`correlated_variance_has_no_canonical_attribution`** | theorem (negative) | with correlation present the two attributions differ — **correlated variance ⇏ unique per-input attribution**; any scheme needs an extra convention |
