## 5. Lane 5 — neighbourhood-sized identifiability — `IdentifiabilityRadius.lean`

From `σ_min(J(θ₀)) ≥ c > 0` and `‖J(θ) − J(θ₀)‖ ≤ L‖θ − θ₀‖`:

| Statement | Kind | Content |
|---|---|---|
| `injOn_ball_of_lipschitz_deriv` | theorem | injectivity on a ball from a uniform lower bound on the derivative |
| **`certifiably_identifiable_within_radius`** | theorem | explicit certified radius `c / (2L)` |
| `locallyIdentifiable_of_certified_radius`, `readings_pin_down_within_the_certified_radius` | theorem | the bridge to the Stage-6 identifiability predicate and to data |
| `quadraticMap_certified_radius` | theorem | a fully worked numeric instance |
| **`certified_radius_is_not_maximal`** | theorem (negative) | the certificate is sufficient, not sharp: injectivity can extend well beyond `c/(2L)` |
| **`no_radius_without_curvature_control`** | theorem (negative) | drop the Lipschitz hypothesis and no radius survives |

This turns "locally identifiable" into "certifiably identifiable within radius
`r`" — with an explicit `r`, and with an explicit statement that `r` is not the
best possible one.

