## 7. The acceptance specimen — `RCCircuit.lean`

`C V̇ = (V_in − V)/R`, closed form `V(t) = V_in + (V₀ − V_in) e^{−t/(RC)}`,
unknowns `(R, C)`, measurements `V(tᵢ)`.

| Statement | Kind | Content |
|---|---|---|
| `rc_solves_the_ode`, `voltage_at_zero` | theorem | the closed form solves the ODE with the right initial condition |
| `voltage_depends_only_on_the_product` | theorem | `(R, C)` enters only through `τ = RC` |
| **`perfect_measurements_do_not_identify_R_and_C`** | theorem (negative) | for **any** dataset, however large and with **zero** tolerance: `(R,C)` compatible ⇒ `(2R, C/2)` compatible and different |
| **`no_dataset_identifies_the_pair`** | theorem (negative) | so the surviving region is never a singleton |
| `vtau_injOn`, `exact_reading_identifies_tau` | theorem | reparameterised by `τ`, one exact reading identifies the parameter |
| `vtau_hasDerivAt_tau` | theorem | certified sensitivity `∂V/∂τ = (V₀−V_in) e^{−t/τ} t/τ²` |
| **`most_informative_time_is_tau`** | theorem | `t e^{−t/τ} ≤ τ e^{−1}` with equality at `t = τ`: sample at one time constant |
| `voltage_hasDerivAt_R`, `voltage_hasDerivAt_C`, `rcJacobian_is_certified` | theorem | the certified `(∂V/∂R, ∂V/∂C)` row of every design |
| **`rc_design_has_an_invisible_direction`** | theorem | `J ·ᵥ (R, −C) = 0` for every multi-time design |
| **`rc_information_is_singular`** | theorem | hence `det F = 0` for every weighting: the lane-3 D-criterion *detects* the obstruction |
| `family_falsified_by_an_impossible_reading` | theorem | the family is nevertheless genuinely refutable |

This is the requested generic antipattern, discovered by the machinery rather
than asserted: **perfect measurements ⇏ parameter identifiability.**

## 8. Theories as experiment-generating objects — `ObservationalEquivalence.lean`

`H₁ ∼_L H₂ ⟺ ∀ e ∈ L, Pred(H₁, e) = Pred(H₂, e)`.

| Statement | Kind | Content |
|---|---|---|
| `Theory`, `ObsEquiv`, `obsClass`, `Separates`, `Refutes`, `Discriminates` | definition | the vocabulary |
| `obsEquiv_equivalence` | theorem | an equivalence relation for each language |
| `obsEquiv_univ_iff`, `obsEquiv_empty` | theorem | the two extremes |
| **`obsEquiv_antitone`, `obsClass_antitone`** | theorem | a larger language is a finer relation: **new experiments refine the observational equivalence classes of theories** |
| `not_obsEquiv_iff_exists_separating` | theorem | inequivalence = a separating experiment inside `L` |
| `obsEquiv_transfers_refutation` / `_survival` | theorem | inside `L`, equivalent theories are one scientific object |
| **`rc_theories_equivalent_for_voltage_only`** | theorem | the RC pairs `(R,C)` and `(2R, C/2)` are observationally equivalent for voltage sampling |
| **`ohmmeter_splits_the_class`, `obsClass_strictly_shrinks`** | theorem | adding one resistance measurement to the language *strictly* shrinks the class |
