The last negative matters for the Stage-9 programme: "choose the measurement
that maximally separates `H₀, H₁, H₂`" is not in general satisfiable by one
measurement.

## 9. Model ladders and held-out validation — `ModelLadder.lean`

A rung is the set of observable time-series a model can produce; `M ≺ M'` is
inclusion of reaches, which is what adding state, parameters or free functions
does.

| Statement | Kind | Content |
|---|---|---|
| `Rung`, `Extends`, `survivors`, `Explains`, `envelopeAt`, `Predicts`, `RisksAt` | definition | the ladder vocabulary |
| `survivors_mono`, `explains_mono`, `refutation_travels_down` | theorem | refutation travels down the ladder, never up |
| `envelopeAt_mono` | theorem | held-out envelopes only widen going up |
| **`added_structure_never_adds_a_held_out_prediction`** | theorem (negative) | everything the richer rung predicts about held-out data, the poorer rung already predicted |
| **`added_structure_can_destroy_the_held_out_prediction`** | theorem (negative) | constants vs. affine on one exact reading: the constant rung forbids a value at `t = 2`, the affine rung forbids nothing |
| `EarnedRung`, `EarnedRung.could_have_failed` | interface / theorem | the only sound step: lower rung refuted, higher rung explains, risks and survives a held-out test |
| `earned_step_is_not_mere_extension` | theorem | if the lower rung still fits, no extension of it is earned |

So the roadmap question — *does added model structure actually improve held-out
prediction?* — has a proved answer for nested ladders: **not by itself.**  It
can only do so via refutation of the lower rung, which is exactly what
`EarnedRung` requires.

